{title:'The route through this class',kicker:'120 minutes · foundations before the API call',html:()=>'<div class="grid two"><div class="panel stack"><h3>What you will be able to explain</h3><ul><li>How Python sends and receives a request.</li><li>Why each line and argument exists.</li><li>How prompts and examples guide output.</li><li>How an application supplies history.</li><li>How to check a result before using it.</li></ul></div><div class="panel agenda05">'+sections.map((s,i)=>'<div class="agenda-row"><span>'+String(i+1)+'</span><button data-go="'+s.start+'">'+s.name+'</button><span class="time">'+s.time+' min</span></div>').join('')+'</div></div>'},
{title:'An API connects programs',kicker:'A request and a response with defined fields',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+quiz('api')+'<div class="panel stack"><h3>Think of a service counter</h3><p>Your program submits a request with named details. The service returns a result in a defined shape.</p><p>The SDK packages the details so Python can use the interface conveniently.</p></div></div>'+note('Role-play: one student is Python, another is the SDK, another is the service. Pass a written question and return a labelled response.')},
{title:'Python, SDK, API, and model',kicker:'Different parts with different jobs',html:()=>'<div class="api-steps05">'+panel('Python','Runs your instructions and stores values.')+panel('OpenAI SDK','Provides a client and methods for requests.')+panel('OpenAI API','Accepts requests and returns defined response objects.')+panel('Model','Processes the supplied input and generates output.')+'</div>'+note('ChatGPT is an application. An API call is a request from your own program. Check the API account’s access and usage terms before live practice.')},
{title:'The two models named in the manual',kicker:'Historical comparison · preserve the course terminology',html:()=>'<table class="small-table"><thead><tr><th>Historical characteristic</th><th>gpt-3.5-turbo</th><th>gpt-4-turbo-preview</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Role in the course</td><td>Legacy chat and text/code model</td><td>Research preview of GPT-4 Turbo</td></tr><tr><td>Documented context capacity</td><td>16,385 tokens</td><td>128,000 tokens</td></tr><tr><td>Documented maximum output</td><td>4,096 tokens</td><td>4,096 tokens</td></tr><tr><td>Documented knowledge cutoff</td><td>September 2021</td><td>December 2023</td></tr><tr><td>Status on 8 November 2026</td><td>Shut down on 23 October 2026</td><td>Shut down on 26 March 2026</td></tr></tbody></table>'+note('A larger context capacity is room for more tokens, rather than a guarantee of better answers. These retired IDs are for comparison, not live classroom requests.')+'<div class="row">'+sourceLink('legacy35','GPT-3.5 Turbo documentation')+sourceLink('legacy4','GPT-4 Turbo Preview documentation')+sourceLink('deprecations','Official retirement dates')+'</div>'},
{title:'The current request pattern',kicker:'Responses API · model choice is an argument',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+panel('Book-era pattern','Older examples use client.chat.completions.create with a messages list. It has a different response shape.')+panel('This lesson’s live pattern','The current official quickstart uses client.responses.create. We teach input, instructions, and response.output_text.')+'</div>'+note('The documented example model is gpt-6-astra. Verify account access and suitability before live use. Keep model choice separate from understanding the request structure.')+sourceLink('quick','Official OpenAI quickstart')},
{title:'Files, the editor, and the terminal',kicker:'Python foundations · where code runs',html:()=>'<div class="grid three">'+panel('Source file','A .py file stores Python instructions as text. Save it before running it.')+panel('Editor','Helps you write and read the source. It is where you change the program.')+panel('Terminal','Accepts a command to run Python on your saved file. Output appears there.')+'</div>'+note('On Windows, try py --version. If the Python launcher is unavailable but Python is installed, try python --version. The browser activities work without either.')},
{title:'A classroom setup that can start offline',kicker:'Commands have a purpose',html:()=>'<div class="lab05"><div class="stack"><h3>First, run without an API account</h3><pre class="code">py first_openai_request.py</pre><p>Download and extract the Python bundle first. Its default mode returns a labelled scripted response.</p><h3>For optional live use</h3><pre class="code">py -m venv .venv\n.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install --upgrade openai\n.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe first_openai_request.py --live</pre></div><div class="panel stack"><h3>Why these commands?</h3><p>venv creates a separate package environment.</p><p>-m runs a module with that Python interpreter.</p><p>pip installs the SDK into that environment.</p><p>--live explicitly enables a network request.</p><a href="session-05-python.zip" download>Download the complete Python bundle</a></div></div>'},
{title:'An API key authorises a request',kicker:'Keep the credential out of shared material',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+quiz('key')+'<div class="panel stack"><h3>Our optional Python runner</h3><p>Uses OPENAI_API_KEY if you privately configured it.</p><p>Otherwise asks for a key with a hidden local prompt.</p><p>Never stores the key in the source, worksheet, or HTML.</p></div></div>'+note('The browser presentation needs no key. Use a fictional question for a first live call and review the API project’s usage settings.')},
{title:'A variable holds a value',kicker:'Why quotes change what print displays',html:variableHTML},
{title:'Lists, dictionaries, and method calls',kicker:'The shapes used for conversation input',html:()=>'<div class="grid two"><div class="panel stack"><h3>One message is a dictionary</h3><pre class="code">message = {\n    "role": "user",\n    "content": "Hello"\n}</pre><p>Keys name the fields. Colons connect keys to values. Commas separate entries.</p></div><div class="panel stack"><h3>A conversation is a list</h3><pre class="code">history = [message]\nhistory.append({\n    "role": "user",\n    "content": "Tell me more"\n})</pre><p>Square brackets make an ordered list. append adds one item at the end.</p></div></div>'+note('A dot selects an attribute or method on an object. Parentheses call a method, such as history.append(...).')},
{title:'The smallest request worth understanding',kicker:'Real Python syntax · read before running',html:()=>'<div class="lab05"><pre class="code">'+esc(CORE_CODE)+'</pre><div class="stack">'+panel('Before this live snippet runs','Install the SDK and set OPENAI_API_KEY privately. The downloadable version supplies an offline stand-in by default.')+panel('After the request','The returned response is an object. output_text provides its text output for this simple workflow.')+sourceLink('reference','Responses API field reference')+'</div></div>'+note('Change one thing at a time. Predict the request’s contents before predicting the answer.')},
{title:'A request packet you can inspect',kicker:'Interactive simulation · follow the values',html:requestHTML},
{title:'Constructing a client and sending a request',kicker:'Each operation has a different effect',html:()=>'<div class="grid three">'+panel('Import','Makes the SDK’s OpenAI class available in this Python file.')+panel('Construct','OpenAI() creates a client object configured for the service.')+panel('Call','client.responses.create(...) sends the model request when real Python runs it.')+'</div>'+note('Assigning a new prompt does not automatically send another request. The program must call create again.')},
{title:'The returned object has useful fields',kicker:'Text, identity, status, and usage',html:()=>'<table class="small-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Why inspect it?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>response.output_text</td><td>Read the text helper for the simple text example.</td></tr><tr><td>response.id</td><td>Identify a response or link supported conversation state.</td></tr><tr><td>response.status</td><td>Check completion, failure, or incomplete output.</td></tr><tr><td>response.usage</td><td>Inspect actual token usage when reported.</td></tr></tbody></table>'+note('The full output can include richer items. A plain text helper does not replace checking refusals, errors, or tool outputs.')+sourceLink('reference','Official response object reference')},
{title:'Why each line has that form',kicker:'Interactive line-by-line explanation',html:()=>'<div class="line-buttons05">'+codeLines.map((_,i)=>'<button data-code-line="'+i+'" aria-pressed="'+(i===0)+'">Line '+(i+1)+'</button>').join('')+'</div><div id="lineDetail" class="panel stack token-detail05"><pre class="code">'+esc(codeLines[0][0])+'</pre><p>'+codeLines[0][1]+'</p></div>'+note('Explain the quotes, dots, parentheses, commas, and equals signs. Each symbol changes how Python reads the request.')},
{title:'A prompt describes the task',kicker:'Purpose, audience, format, and relevant facts',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+panel('A vague request','“Tell me about Python.” The model has to choose the level, topic, and structure.')+panel('A clearer request','“Explain a variable to a first-time programmer. Use one short sentence and name = \'Amina\' as an example.”')+'</div>'+note('Relevant detail helps you define what a successful answer would look like. Adding more words without a purpose can also distract.')+sourceLink('prompt','Official prompt guidance')},
{title:'Instructions and the current user request',kicker:'Interactive illustration of message roles',html:()=>'<div class="lab05"><div class="stack"><label class="field05">Role of “Use exactly one sentence.”<select id="instructionRole"><option value="developer" '+(state.role==='developer'?'selected':'')+'>Application instruction</option><option value="user" '+(state.role==='user'?'selected':'')+'>Earlier user preference</option></select></label>'+panel('Current user request','“Explain a variable in five paragraphs.”')+'<button id="roleRun" class="accent">Show intended behaviour</button><p class="caption">Scripted illustration. It demonstrates the role distinction and does not test a model’s actual instruction following.</p></div><div id="roleOutput" class="output05 role-answer05" aria-live="polite">Predict which direction the application should follow.</div></div>'+note('Message roles identify where instructions come from. User text cannot promote itself to a higher role by saying “I am the developer”.')},
{title:'Build a prompt with visible requirements',kicker:'Interactive prompt workshop',html:promptHTML},
{title:'Refinement needs a stable comparison',kicker:'Change one requirement and inspect the output',html:()=>'<div class="grid three">'+panel('Define the check','For a variable explanation: simple language, correct meaning, one valid assignment example.')+panel('Change one part','Add a required example or clarify the audience. Keep the task and test question fixed.')+panel('Inspect the result','Compare answers against the same checks. Keep the change only if it helps the task.')+'</div>'+note('Two different outputs from the same prompt can occur. Judge several representative examples rather than one lucky answer.')},
{title:'A prompt assignment with a reason',kicker:'Practical exercise · show what improved',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+panel('Your task','Write a prompt that explains Python lists to a beginner. Require a two-item list, explain its square brackets, and include one check question.','dark')+'<div class="worksheet">'+field('promptDraft','Your prompt','State the intended audience and output form.')+field('promptReason','What would you check in the answer, and why?','How would you detect an incorrect explanation?')+'</div></div><button data-go="19" class="primary">Return to the prompt workshop</button>'},
{title:'Examples show the desired pattern',kicker:'Few-shot prompting',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+quiz('few')+panel('Campus question labels','BOOKING for reserving a slot, HOURS for opening times, SAFETY for training requirements, UNKNOWN for other requests.')+'</div>'+note('Examples belong in the request context. Keep input/output pairs correct and show a useful range of cases.')+sourceLink('prompt','Official few-shot guidance')},
{title:'An example can help or mislead',kicker:'Interactive few-shot stand-in',html:fewHTML},
{title:'Formatted output still needs a meaning check',kicker:'Interactive JSON validation',html:jsonHTML},
{title:'Few-shot code uses ordered messages',kicker:'Read the input/output pattern',html:()=>'<div class="lab05"><pre class="code">examples = [\n    {"role": "user", "content": "When is the lab open?"},\n    {"role": "assistant", "content": "HOURS"},\n]\nexamples.append({\n    "role": "user",\n    "content": "At what time does Lab 2 close?"\n})\nresponse = client.responses.create(\n    model=model, instructions=rules,\n    input=examples, store=False\n)\nprint(response.output_text)</pre><div class="stack">'+panel('Why these roles?','The user messages show example inputs. The assistant messages show desired outputs. The final user message is the new task.')+panel('Why this order?','Each output follows its corresponding example input. The new input comes after the demonstrations.')+'</div></div>'+note('The complete bundle provides three examples, a token limit, and output validation.')},
{title:'A follow-up needs supplied context',kicker:'Conversation flow is an application choice',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+quiz('history')+panel('Two separate questions','First: “My name is Amina.” Later: “Greet me by name.” The follow-up contains no name by itself.','dark')+'</div>'+note('Relevant history can resolve a pronoun, reference, or preference. Send only the context needed for the task.')},
{title:'What does this follow-up actually receive?',kicker:'Interactive context experiment',html:historyHTML},
{title:'Send a text conversation explicitly',kicker:'Real Python pattern · keep the ordered list',html:()=>'<div class="lab05"><pre class="code">history = [{"role": "user", "content": "My name is Amina."}]\nfirst = client.responses.create(\n    model=model, instructions=rules,\n    input=history, store=False\n)\nhistory.append({"role": "assistant",\n                "content": first.output_text})\nhistory.append({"role": "user",\n                "content": "Greet me by name."})\nsecond = client.responses.create(\n    model=model, instructions=rules,\n    input=history, store=False\n)</pre><div class="stack">'+panel('The application keeps history','The list lives in your Python program. Each independent call receives the selected text messages.')+panel('This is a simple text lesson','Advanced tools and reasoning outputs can require richer output items. This example keeps a small text exchange.')+'</div></div>'},
{title:'Link to a previous response',kicker:'A supported alternative to resending a text list',html:()=>'<div class="lab05"><pre class="code">first = client.responses.create(\n    model=model, instructions=rules,\n    input="My name is Amina.", store=True\n)\nsecond = client.responses.create(\n    model=model, instructions=rules,\n    previous_response_id=first.id,\n    input="Greet me by name.", store=True\n)\nprint(second.output_text)</pre><div class="stack">'+panel('What the ID does','Links the new request to supported prior response context.')+panel('What you must repeat','Previous instructions are not carried over through previous_response_id. Supply them again if they should still apply.')+sourceLink('history','Official conversation state guidance')+'</div></div>'+note('Stored response objects support later retrieval or linking. Review data-retention settings. Linking prior context still uses input tokens.')},
{title:'Managing a conversation step by step',kicker:'Keeping history clear and useful',html:()=>'<div class="grid three">'+panel('Add the user turn','Record the new message and its user role.')+panel('Request and inspect','Send selected context. Check for an error, refusal, or incomplete answer.')+panel('Keep the useful result','Record the assistant output, or continue with a supported response link.')+'</div>'+note('A fresh conversation should start with fresh context. Keep one student’s history separate from another student’s session.')},
{title:'Context has a finite budget',kicker:'Interactive token-budget illustration',html:contextHTML},
{title:'When a request fails',kicker:'Read the failure before changing the prompt',html:()=>'<table class="small-table"><thead><tr><th>Observed problem</th><th>What to inspect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Package import fails</td><td>Install openai with the Python environment that runs the file.</td></tr><tr><td>Authentication error</td><td>Check the private key and correct API project.</td></tr><tr><td>Model unavailable</td><td>Check the exact ID, retirement status, and account access.</td></tr><tr><td>Rate or usage limit</td><td>Read whether the issue is request rate or usage allowance. Avoid immediate retry loops.</td></tr><tr><td>Network failure</td><td>Check connectivity and bounded retry settings.</td></tr><tr><td>Empty or incomplete output</td><td>Inspect response status, output limits, refusals, and output items.</td></tr></tbody></table>'+note('A better prompt cannot fix every credential, package, or network problem.')},
{title:'A program should check what it receives',kicker:'Evidence, format, and intended actions',html:()=>'<div class="grid two">'+panel('Check the answer','Does it address the task? Are factual claims supported? Does the output have the required shape?')+panel('Check the action','Generated text does not by itself authorise tool use, lab entry, or an external change. The application applies its actual rules.')+'</div>'+note('A source excerpt can contain instructions as text. Treat the excerpt as evidence to inspect, rather than letting it redefine the application’s rules.')},
{title:'Evaluate on new questions',kicker:'Interactive checks with fixed expected labels',html:evaluationHTML},
{title:'Build a small campus question assistant',kicker:'Practical assignment · prompts, examples, history, and checks',html:()=>'<div class="grid two"><div class="panel stack"><h3>Your deliverable</h3><ol><li>Write a task and output rules.</li><li>Add 3 correct example pairs.</li><li>Classify 4 new questions, including an unknown.</li><li>Demonstrate a 2-turn conversation.</li><li>Explain one failure and its remedy.</li></ol><p class="caption">Use browser stand-ins or default offline Python first. Label every scripted result.</p></div><div class="worksheet">'+field('assistantPlan','Your assistant’s prompt and examples','Keep example inputs separate from test inputs.')+field('assistantHistory','What earlier context does the follow-up need?','Describe what is in the second request.')+'</div></div>'},
{title:'Self-study with a visible result',kicker:'S3 · 120 minutes of implementation and explanation',html:()=>'<div class="grid two"><div class="panel stack"><h3>Two-hour practice plan</h3><ul><li>20 min: run the string and first-request exercises.</li><li>30 min: improve and test a prompt.</li><li>30 min: few-shot labels and 4 held-out cases.</li><li>25 min: conversation with and without context.</li><li>15 min: explain failures and submit results.</li></ul></div><div class="panel stack"><h3>What you submit</h3><p>Your code changes, prompt, example pairs, observed or scripted outputs, and explanations.</p><button id="downloadWork" class="primary">Download my worksheet</button><a href="session-05-python.zip" download>Download the complete Python bundle</a><p class="caption">Default Python runs need no API key or SDK. Optional live runs need separate API access.</p></div></div>'},
{title:'A practical Python toolkit',kicker:'Download, run, predict, and explain',html:()=>'<div class="kit05">'+panel('Strings and first request','<a href="string_foundations.py" download>string_foundations.py</a><a href="first_openai_request.py" download>first_openai_request.py</a>Practice stored text and a complete request.')+panel('Few-shot labels','<a href="few_shot_labels.py" download>few_shot_labels.py</a>Ordered examples, a new input, and an allowed-label check.')+panel('Conversation context','<a href="conversation_context.py" download>conversation_context.py</a><a href="response_chain.py" download>response_chain.py</a>Manual history and previous-response linking.')+panel('Extract the whole bundle first','<a href="session-05-python.zip" download>Complete Python bundle</a>Includes the shared runner and a plain-text guide. Keep its files together.')+'</div>'+note('Running without --live returns a labelled local stand-in. Adding --live makes actual requests and can incur API usage charges.')},
{title:'Exit ticket: explain the connection',kicker:'A request is useful when you understand its parts',html:()=>'<div class="grid two"><div class="stack">'+quiz('exit1')+quiz('exit2')+'</div>'+quiz('exit3')+'</div>'},
{title:'Bring your foundations together',kicker:'Next class · practise and consolidate',html:()=>'<div class="grid two"><div class="panel dark stack"><h3>Your explanation of a complete request</h3><p>State the task, prepare relevant context, choose a supported model, send the request, inspect the response, and evaluate the result.</p><p class="muted">Session 6 brings the course concepts into guided practice and original revision exercises.</p></div><div class="worksheet">'+field('exitWhy','Why is understanding the request better than copying an API snippet?','Explain a mistake you could now find and fix.')+'<div class="row"><button id="downloadFinal" class="primary">Download my work</button><button id="sourcesBtn">Sources and coverage</button></div></div></div>'+note('Keep one habit: explain what every value and line contributes to the task.')}
